Z||000000||BurlyGoat||08-30-2001||09:08 AM||||Hey,<P>Just need confirmation. The two relays on the firewall (behind the air filter) are for the turn signals correct ? If not please take me out back and........<P>Also, where exactly is this idle air screw on the throttle body ?<P>Thanks||||reg||10
Z||000001||BOILERMAKER||08-30-2001||10:20 AM||||I thought they were for AC and fuel pump.  I could be wrong, but that is what I remember.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||08-30-2001||10:40 AM||||The two relays are for the fuel pump (rightmost) and the coolant fan relay (leftmost). If your car was equiped with AC from the factory you will have a third relay for the compressor which they wisely put in the middle so they could simplify documentation on the relays.  Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BurlyGoat||08-30-2001||10:55 AM||||Thanks for the replies, I feel kinda stupid, but I found out what they were just after I posted it....Funny how my car takes alot to start because the fuel relay lines were corroded and broken. I guess the oil pump runs the fuel pump after the oil pressure reaches 4psi. Could anyone tell me about the idle screws location ?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||08-30-2001||01:35 PM||||idle air control adjustment screws are usually capped off from the factory.  There should be no reason to adjust this.  If you have a high idle check for vacuum leaks, especially the EGR tube if this is a V6.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BurlyGoat||08-31-2001||09:02 AM||||Does anyone have the wiring diagram for the fuel relay. I removed the bolt and 3 wires snapped off immediately. Unfortunately there is no wire insulator left in the connector to see where these wires go. It is a 86se V6.<P>Thanks||||reg||11
Z||000006||maryjane||08-31-2001||11:05 AM||||I just emailed you the wiring diagram for the v-6 fuel system, includes the relay wiring. Also, someone had posted a couple of links to some good wiring diagrams for Fiero a few weeks ago. I meant to bookmark them, but didn't. Maybe they will read your question and repost. You may try looking in search, couldn't have been more than 2 wks ago, cna't remember what the original subject was.<BR>Don <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-31-2001||08:00 PM||||Never mess with the idle stop screw. If your idle is off the stop screw is not your problem. Vacuum leaks and static timing are the biggest culprits there...||||reg||1
